hould that \"next backtick\" skip escaped backticks? For example `` `a \\` b` ``? Positions: 0 `` ` ``, 1 `a`, 2 ` `, 3 `\\`, 4 `` ` ``, 5 ` `, 6 `b`, 7 `` ` ``. Main loop: position 0 backtick, look for next backtick: position 4 (escaped) or position 7?\n\nHmm. R5 says \"A backtick opens a code span that ends at the next backtick.\" Inside a code span there's no backslash processing. The question is whether the opener search sees escaped backticks. I think the natural reading of left-to-right parsing is: at position 0 we see a backtick and look for \"the next backtick\" — the next backtick character in the text. The backslash at position 3 is \"elsewhere\" (not inside a code span, not followed by an escapable character... wait, it IS followed by a backtick, which IS escapable!).\n\nUgh. R7: \"A backslash followed by ... a backtick ... outputs that second character as plain text.\" So in normal (non-code) text, `\\`` → plain `` ` ``. So in the main loop, position 3 `\\` + position 4 `` ` `` gets consumed as a pair. So when the backtick at position 0 opens a code span, \"ends at the next backtick\" — the next backtick that's part of the parse stream, i.e., not escaped. So the code span ends at position 7, and the content is `a \\` b` — but inside a code span, \"no backslash processing\" → the content is rendered as `a \\` b` with only &<> escaping.\n\nSo the search for the closing backtick must account for escape pairing: walk from i+1, and at each position if it's `\\` with an escapable character following, skip both; if it's a backtick, stop.\n\nAlternatively, \"the next backtick\" is simply the next literal backtick character, regardless of escaping. Hmm. Which is correct?\n\nLet me re-read R5: \"A backtick opens a code span that ends at the next backtick. Inside it, only the R1 escapes apply: no markup, no backslash processing. A backtick with no subsequent backtick is plain text. Two consecutive backticks with nothing between are plain text, as two backticks.\"\n